#N canvas 40 60 1280 1210 12;
#X declare -path ..;
#X obj 0 0 cnv 1 1280 46 empty empty 20\ \ \ Radial\ basis\ functions\ \ -\ \ bumps\ that\ morph\ between\ presets\ (1988-89) 14 23 0 20 #1d3557 #ffffff 0;
#X obj 1110 1180 declare -path ..;
#X text 800 1180 finds the nn.* objects one folder up ->, f 42;
#X text 20 1180 neuralplay - Moisés Horta Valenzuela / SEMILLA.AI Studio, f 70;
#X text 20 56 Six presets sit on a pad as the hidden units of a radial basis function network: each one is a bump \, and the six outputs are sound parameters. Move between the presets and the network morphs the sound., f 175;
#X obj 10 96 cnv 1 390 864 empty empty THE\ IDEA:\ bumps\ instead\ of\ steps 8 12 0 13 #eef1f5 #1d3557 0;
#X text 20 122 Lesson 8 mapped points to sounds with sigmoid units: smooth steps that stretch across the whole input. A radial basis function (RBF) network uses BUMPS instead. Each hidden unit sits at a point \, its centre \, and fires most when the input is near it: exp(-d^2 / 2 sigma^2) \, d = the distance to the centre (Broomhead and Lowe \, 1988)., f 52;
#X text 20 248 HERE the input is a point on the pad \, the six centres are your presets and the six outputs are the parameters of a synth. Moving the cursor morphs the sound: a few hand-made sounds and a continuous space between them \, as in many synths and live set-ups., f 52;
#X text 20 344 NORMALISED (Moody and Darken \, 1989): every bump is divided by the sum of all six \, so the hidden layer says how much of each preset you hear - the bars \, and the colours of the pad. The width of bump j: sigma_j = width x the distance to its nearest neighbour., f 52;
#X text 20 440 THE OUTPUT LAYER is a linear associator: output i = sum_j g_j z_ji. With z = the presets themselves the network blends them. But at a preset its neighbours still leak in: you do not hear the preset you stored (the error at the presets)., f 52;
#X text 20 536 THE DELTA RULE fixes that (Rojas 16.2.5). The presets are the training examples and the error nudges the weights: Delta z_j = gamma g_j (f - output). Round after round \, until every preset comes out exactly - the network now interpolates., f 52;
#X text 20 632 THE CATCH: wide bumps overlap \, and hitting every preset exactly then needs big weights of opposite sign. Between the presets the sound overshoots \, and training crawls. Narrow bumps are exact at once \, but the pad breaks into tiles: the nearest preset wins \, like the codebook of lesson 18 (vector quantization). Good morphing lives in between., f 52;
#X text 20 758 Rojas puts RBF networks among the hybrid networks: a Kohonen layer (lesson 18) could learn the centres from data. Here you place them by hand., f 52;
#X obj 410 96 cnv 1 470 594 empty empty THE\ MORPH\ PAD\ \ (the\ input:\ a\ point) 8 12 0 13 #e3f0fb #1d3557 0;
#X text 430 120 Dots = your presets. Colours = which preset each spot belongs to \, mixed where they share it (the hidden layer)., f 64;
